WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
How to use this elective.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:07.533
This elective walks you through Andrej Karpathy’s “Neural Networks: Zero to Hero” lectures.

3
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:12.037
Each lesson is a companion to one video (or, this week, half of one).

4
00:00:12.317 --> 00:00:24.115
The pattern is the same every time: watch the video with a few things to look for, read the math written out properly, rebuild one piece in pure Python right here in the browser, and check yourself.

5
00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:28.192
You can return to the lessons later as a review sheet without rewatching.

6
00:00:28.472 --> 00:00:35.427
Karpathy’s lectures assume “a vague recollection of calculus from high school,” in his words on the syllabus page.

7
00:00:35.707 --> 00:00:48.677
You have much more than that, so these notes lean into the math: they state the chain rule precisely, derive the gradients the video computes by hand, and explain why the engineering choices follow from the calculus.

8
00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:53.459
Why should someone building on Claude or GPT spend 30 hours on this?

9
00:00:53.739 --> 00:01:07.627
Because nearly every behavior you deal with as an applied engineer (tokens, context windows, sampling, why fine-tuning shifts behavior, why a loss curve is or is not good news) traces back to what you will build here.

10
00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:09.592
You won’t train production models.

11
00:01:09.872 --> 00:01:17.403
You will be the person on the team who can read a model card, a fine-tuning report, or a paper and tell what it actually claims.
